        sectionType: "integrate",
        title: "SDA Theological Integration",
        content: `The Adventist affirmation of the Trinity rests on biblical evidence rather than creedal inheritance. Several key distinctives mark the Adventist position:

**A doctrine arrived at, not inherited.** Early Adventist pioneers included individuals from various theological backgrounds, some of whom questioned the Trinity. Through decades of sustained Bible study \u2014 particularly concerning the full divinity of Christ and the personhood of the Holy Spirit \u2014 the movement arrived at a trinitarian conviction on its own terms. This history reminds Adventists that doctrinal integrity requires following Scripture wherever it leads, even beyond inherited assumptions.

**Three Persons, not three roles.** Adventists reject modalism \u2014 the idea that God is one Person who appears in three different "modes" (like an actor wearing three masks). The baptism of Jesus (Matthew 3:16-17) demonstrates three simultaneous, distinct Persons: the Son in the water, the Spirit descending, and the Father speaking from heaven. Each Person is conscious, relational, and fully divine.

**The Great Controversy and trinitarian cooperation.** The cosmic conflict between Christ and Satan is resolved through the cooperative work of all three Persons. The Father initiates the plan of redemption, the Son executes it through incarnation and atonement, and the Spirit applies it through conviction, regeneration, and empowerment. Satan's rebellion was ultimately a rejection of the relational order within the Godhead \u2014 he sought to displace the Son (Isaiah 14:13-14) and fracture the unity of heaven.

**One divine essence, three Persons.** Adventists affirm that the Father, Son, and Holy Spirit share the same divine nature and attributes \u2014 omniscience, omnipotence, omnipresence, eternality. There is no hierarchy of essence, though there is a voluntary order of function within the plan of salvation.`,
