        title: "What Does This Mean?",
        content: `Isaiah 44:6 declares in the strongest possible terms that there is only one God. The LORD (Yahweh) identifies Himself as "the First and the Last" \u2014 a title later applied to Jesus in Revelation 1:17 and 22:13. This creates a theological tension that the doctrine of the Trinity resolves: if the Father is the only God and Jesus also bears the title that belongs to God alone, then they must share the same divine nature.

Jesus' statement in John 10:30 \u2014 "I and My Father are one" \u2014 was understood by His hearers as a claim to deity. They immediately took up stones to execute Him for blasphemy, saying, "You, being a Man, make Yourself God" (v. 33). Jesus did not correct their understanding of His claim; He defended it. The Greek word hen ("one") is neuter, suggesting not "one Person" but "one thing" \u2014 one in essence, nature, and purpose.

Peter's confrontation with Ananias in Acts 5:3-4 provides decisive evidence for the deity of the Holy Spirit. In verse 3, Peter says Ananias lied "to the Holy Spirit." In verse 4, he says Ananias lied "to God." The equation is direct: the Holy Spirit is God. This is not a figure of speech or a loose identification; Peter treats lying to the Spirit and lying to God as the same act.

Three common errors arise from misreading these texts. Modalism collapses the three Persons into one Person wearing three masks \u2014 but John 10:30 distinguishes "I" and "My Father" as two while affirming their oneness. Tritheism splits the Godhead into three separate Gods \u2014 but Isaiah 44:6 insists there is only one. Subordinationism denies the full deity of the Son or Spirit \u2014 but Acts 5:3-4 identifies the Spirit as God, and Revelation applies the Father's own title to the Son.`,
