        content: `Exodus 34:6-7 stands as the single most quoted Old Testament passage within the Old Testament itself. When Moses asked to see God's glory, the Lord responded not with a display of raw power but with a verbal self-portrait: "The LORD, the LORD God, merciful and gracious, longsuffering, and abounding in goodness and truth." The Hebrew word chesed \u2014 rendered "goodness" or "lovingkindness" \u2014 describes a loyal, covenant love that persists even when the other party falters. This is who God says He is when He defines Himself.

Notice the sequence: mercy comes before judgment. Grace precedes the warning about iniquity. The Father does not grudgingly forgive; forgiveness flows from His essential nature. Yet the passage also affirms that God "will by no means clear the guilty." Justice and mercy are not opposites in the Father's character \u2014 they are partners. He takes sin seriously precisely because He loves deeply.

Paul's hymn in 1 Timothy 1:17 adds another dimension: the Father is "the King eternal, immortal, invisible." He transcends time, decay, and human sight. This is not a God we can reduce to a comfortable image. His sovereignty is absolute \u2014 yet it is the sovereignty of One whose defining trait is chesed.

Jesus sharpened this portrait decisively in John 14:9. Philip asked to see the Father, and Jesus replied that anyone who had seen Him had already seen the Father. The Father is not hiding behind the Son; the Son is the visible expression of the Father's character. Every act of healing, every word of pardon, every meal with sinners \u2014 these are the Father's own dispositions made tangible in human form.`,
