        title: "SDA Theological Integration",
        content: `The Adventist doctrine of Christ's nature carries direct implications for the sanctuary and judgment teachings. In the heavenly sanctuary, Christ serves as our High Priest \u2014 a role that requires genuine humanity (Hebrews 2:17). He does not plead our case as a distant deity; He intercedes as one who wore our flesh, bore our sorrows, and conquered our temptations. His mediation is experiential, not theoretical.

Adventists have historically discussed the precise nature of Christ's human flesh \u2014 whether He took Adam's pre-fall or post-fall nature. While the church has not issued a dogmatic ruling on this question, the biblical emphasis is clear: Christ was "in all points tempted as we are, yet without sin" (Hebrews 4:15). Whatever position one holds, two boundaries must be maintained: Christ's temptations were real (not staged), and He never sinned (not even internally).

The Great Controversy framework adds urgency to this doctrine. Satan argued that God's law cannot be kept, that obedience is impossible. By living a fully human life of complete obedience, Jesus answered that charge. He demonstrated that a human being, dependent on the Father and empowered by the Spirit, can reflect God's character \u2014 and He did so under conditions far harder than Eden.

1 Timothy 2:5 also undergirds the Adventist rejection of any earthly priestly mediator between God and the believer. Christ alone holds that office. No human institution, no saint, no ritual can substitute for His direct, personal intercession. This conviction shapes Adventist worship: we come directly to the Father through the Son, without human intermediaries.`,
