        sectionType: "explain",
        title: "What Does This Mean?",
        content: `Paul's declaration in Galatians 2:20 is among the most radical statements in Scripture. "I have been crucified with Christ" \u2014 the old self, with its enslaving patterns and self-centered ambitions, has been put to death. "It is no longer I who live, but Christ lives in me" \u2014 the believer's identity is no longer self-constructed but Christ-inhabited. This is not mystical absorption; Paul immediately adds, "the life which I now live in the flesh I live by faith in the Son of God, who loved me and gave Himself for me." The Christian still lives a concrete, embodied life \u2014 but the animating principle has changed from self to Christ.

This inner transformation produces outward results. 2 Corinthians 5:17 announces: "If anyone is in Christ, he is a new creation; old things have passed away; behold, all things have become new." Paul does not say "improved" or "upgraded" \u2014 he says "new creation." The language deliberately echoes Genesis. What God did at the dawn of time \u2014 calling something out of nothing \u2014 He does again in the life of every person who is united to Christ. And this new creation carries a mission: God has given us "the ministry of reconciliation" (v. 18). Those who have been reconciled to God now carry that message to others.

Hebrews 4:15-16 assures us that this life of faith is not a solo endeavor. We have a High Priest who "was in all points tempted as we are, yet without sin." Because of this, we can "come boldly to the throne of grace, that we may obtain mercy and find grace to help in time of need." The throne is not a courtroom bench before which we cower; it is a throne of grace to which we are invited \u2014 boldly.`,
