        content: `Adventist theology holds together what some traditions separate: justification (God's declaration that we are righteous in Christ) and sanctification (the Spirit's ongoing work of transforming our character). Galatians 2:20 captures this unity. "I have been crucified with Christ" speaks to the definitive break with sin's dominion \u2014 justification. "Christ lives in me" describes the ongoing reality of sanctification \u2014 Christ's character being formed within the believer.

This is not salvation by works. The entire process is powered by grace received through faith. But Adventists resist any theology that treats justification as the whole story and sanctification as optional. The new creation of 2 Corinthians 5:17 is real, not merely positional. Regenerated lives bear visible fruit: changed habits, restored relationships, service to others.

The "ministry of reconciliation" (2 Corinthians 5:18-19) connects directly to the Adventist sense of mission. The church exists not for itself but to carry the Father's reconciling love to every nation, tribe, and tongue. Health ministries, education, community service, and the proclamation of the three angels' messages all flow from the conviction that God has entrusted this ministry to His people.

Hebrews 4:15-16 undergirds the Adventist sanctuary teaching. The heavenly High Priest is not a distant theological concept; He is an active, sympathetic intercessor who understands human struggle from the inside. Coming "boldly to the throne of grace" is not presumption \u2014 it is the privilege purchased by Christ's blood and sustained by His ongoing ministry.`,
