        title: "What Does This Mean?",
        content: `Several persistent misunderstandings about the Son circulate in popular religious thought. Addressing them directly strengthens faith and sharpens witness.

The first error reduces Jesus to a great moral teacher but denies His deity. This position cannot survive contact with Jesus' own claims. In John 5:22-23, Jesus states that the Father "has committed all judgment to the Son, that all should honor the Son just as they honor the Father." Receiving the same honor as the Father is a claim no mere teacher could make without blasphemy. Jesus did not leave the "good teacher" option open; He claimed divine prerogatives and accepted worship.

A second error treats salvation as something humans earn through moral effort, with Jesus serving merely as an example. Romans 6:23 dismantles this: "The wages of sin is death, but the gift of God is eternal life in Christ Jesus our Lord." Eternal life is a gift, not a wage. The problem of sin is not a behavioral deficit that better effort can fix; it is a death sentence that only divine intervention can reverse. Jesus is not merely a model to imitate but a Savior who accomplished what no human effort could achieve.

A third misunderstanding separates Jesus from the Old Testament, as if He appears only in Matthew through Revelation. Luke 24:25-27 corrects this powerfully. On the road to Emmaus, the risen Christ "beginning at Moses and all the Prophets, expounded to them in all the Scriptures the things concerning Himself." The entire Old Testament \u2014 law, prophets, psalms \u2014 points to and finds its fulfillment in the Son. He is not a New Testament addition; He is the subject of the whole Bible.

A fourth error treats Jesus' sacrifice as a transaction that changed the Father's mind \u2014 as though the cross persuaded an angry God to forgive. Scripture tells a different story. God "so loved the world that He gave His only begotten Son" (John 3:16). The cross originated in the Father's love, not in spite of it. Father and Son acted together in the plan of salvation from before the foundation of the world.`,
