        id: "w1s-5-2-3",
        lessonId: "w1l-5-2",
        sectionType: "integrate",
        title: "SDA Theological Integration",
        content: `Adventist theology recognizes four primary spheres of the Spirit's ministry: inspiration, conviction, sanctification, and empowerment.

First, the Spirit inspired Scripture and continues to illuminate its meaning to receptive hearts. The Adventist commitment to sola scriptura rests on the conviction that the same Spirit who inspired the biblical writers guides faithful readers today \u2014 not to add new doctrine, but to unfold what is already written.

Second, the Spirit convicts of sin, righteousness, and judgment (John 16:8-11). This conviction is not condemnation but an invitation. Within the Great Controversy, the Spirit's conviction counters Satan's accusations by pointing hearts toward Christ's finished work rather than toward despair.

Third, sanctification \u2014 the Spirit's ongoing work of transforming character \u2014 is central to Adventist spirituality. Righteousness by faith includes not only justification (Christ's righteousness credited to us) but also sanctification (Christ's righteousness reproduced in us through the Spirit). Both are gifts of grace, received by faith.

Fourth, spiritual gifts equip the church for mission. Adventists affirm the continuing validity of all biblical gifts, including the gift of prophecy, which has found particular expression in the ministry of Ellen G. White. The test for any claimed gift remains scriptural: does it glorify Christ, edify the body, and align with the written Word? For a concise summary, search "spiritual gifts" at egwwritings.org.`,
