        title: "SDA Theological Integration",
        content: `Adventist spirituality places the Spirit's fruit and power squarely within the Great Controversy narrative. Satan's kingdom is characterized by the works of the flesh \u2014 hatred, discord, jealousy, rage, selfish ambition (Galatians 5:19-21). The Spirit's fruit is the visible evidence that God's kingdom has taken root in a human heart. Every act of patience under provocation, every moment of genuine kindness toward an enemy, is a small but real victory in the cosmic conflict.

The Adventist understanding of the "latter rain" connects directly to the filling described in Ephesians 5:18 and the power promised in Acts 1:8. Just as the early rain fell at Pentecost to launch the church's mission, the latter rain will fall to complete it. This final outpouring is not arbitrary; it comes to those who have cooperated with the Spirit in character development during the "early rain" period. The fruit must ripen before the harvest.

Practically, Adventists have historically connected Spirit-filled living with health reform, Sabbath faithfulness, and community service \u2014 not as legalistic requirements, but as arenas where the Spirit's fruit becomes tangible. A body cared for with discipline (self-control), a Sabbath observed with delight (joy and peace), a neighbor served without fanfare (kindness and goodness) \u2014 these are the Spirit's fruit on display.

For further reading on the latter rain and character preparation, search "latter rain" at egwwritings.org in The Acts of the Apostles, chapters 4-5.`,
