        title: "SDA Theological Integration",
        content: `Adventist history provides instructive case studies for navigating these challenges. During the 1890s-1900s, the "holy flesh" movement in Indiana promoted extreme emotional manifestations as evidence of the Spirit's baptism. Church leaders, guided by Ellen G. White's counsel, rejected these excesses \u2014 not because they doubted the Spirit's power, but because the fruit did not align with Scripture. The test was always the same: does this produce the character of Christ?

At the same time, Adventists have been equally cautious about the opposite error. A formal, intellectual faith that resists the Spirit's transforming power is just as far from the biblical ideal as emotionalism. The 1888 emphasis on righteousness by faith \u2014 championed by A. T. Jones and E. J. Waggoner \u2014 was in part a call to stop quenching the Spirit's work of bringing Christ's living presence into the believer's daily experience.

Within the Great Controversy, Satan's strategy is twofold: either counterfeit the Spirit's work with false manifestations, or suppress it with cold formalism. Both achieve the same result \u2014 a church unable to reflect God's character to the watching universe. The Adventist calling is to walk the narrow path between these extremes: open to everything the Spirit genuinely does, yet testing every claim by the Word He Himself inspired.

For further reading on testing spiritual manifestations, search "fanaticism" and "holy flesh" at egwwritings.org in Selected Messages, Book 2.`,
