        lessonId: "w1l-6-1",
        sectionType: "integrate",
        title: "SDA Theological Integration",
        content: `For Seventh-day Adventists, the creation account is not a peripheral doctrine \u2014 it is foundational to the entire theological system. The Sabbath, which defines Adventist identity, derives its meaning directly from the creation week. If creation did not occur in six literal days followed by a seventh-day rest, the Sabbath commandment loses its historical anchor.

The Great Controversy framework places creation at the very beginning of the cosmic conflict. God created a perfect world; Satan introduced rebellion and ruin. The entire plan of redemption is, in essence, God's project of re-creation \u2014 restoring what sin has damaged. This is why the final message to the world in Revelation 14:7 calls humanity to "worship Him who made the heavens, the earth, the sea, and the springs of water." The first angel's message is a creation appeal.

Adventists also connect creation to the doctrine of wholistic humanity. Genesis 2:7 describes God forming Adam from the dust and breathing into him the breath of life \u2014 the result is a "living being" (nephesh chayyah), an indivisible unity of matter and spirit. This understanding shapes the Adventist view of death, resurrection, and health \u2014 the whole person matters because the whole person was created by God.

For further study on creation and the Sabbath, search "creation" at egwwritings.org in Patriarchs and Prophets, chapters 2-3.`,
