        title: "SDA Theological Integration",
        content: `Seventh-day Adventists affirm a recent, literal, six-day creation as described in Genesis 1. This is not simply a scientific claim \u2014 it is a theological conviction with far-reaching implications. The 2004 General Conference statement on creation and the subsequent affirmation at the 2010 General Conference session reaffirmed the church's commitment to the plain reading of Genesis.

Why does this matter so deeply? Because the creation account undergirds several interconnected doctrines. The Sabbath requires a literal creation week. Marriage requires a historical Adam and Eve. The fall requires a real transition from perfection to sin. The atonement requires a genuine problem (the entry of death through sin) with a genuine solution (Christ's life, death, and resurrection). Remove the historical foundation, and these doctrines become metaphors rather than realities.

Within the Great Controversy, creation establishes God's rightful sovereignty. Satan's rebellion was, at its core, a challenge to God's authority as Creator. The first angel's message (Revelation 14:7) calls the world to "worship Him who made" \u2014 a direct echo of the Fourth Commandment. In the final conflict, creation is not a side issue; it is the central issue. To acknowledge God as Creator is to acknowledge His right to define reality, morality, and destiny.

Adventists also affirm that faith and genuine scientific inquiry are compatible. Hebrews 11:3 does not dismiss observation; it clarifies that ultimate origins lie beyond what observation alone can reach. The church supports scientific research while maintaining that Scripture is the authoritative framework for interpreting origins.`,
