        title: "SDA Theological Integration",
        content: `Adventists have engaged the creation question with sustained seriousness throughout their history. The Geoscience Research Institute, established in 1958, reflects the church's commitment to thoughtful dialogue between Scripture and the natural sciences. The institute does not view faith and scientific inquiry as enemies; rather, it seeks to understand the natural world within the framework Scripture provides.

Within the Great Controversy, the creation doctrine carries eschatological urgency. The first angel's message of Revelation 14:7 \u2014 "worship Him who made" \u2014 is a direct challenge to any worldview that removes God from the origin story. In the final conflict, the question is not merely "How old is the earth?" but "Who has the right to define reality \u2014 the Creator, or the creature?" Sabbath observance becomes the visible, weekly answer to that question.

Adventists also recognize that humility is essential when discussing origins. The creation account tells us what God did and why it matters, but it does not answer every scientific question. Genesis was not written as a geology textbook; it was written as a theological declaration. Insisting on details the text does not address is as problematic as denying the truths it clearly affirms.

The Adventist position can be summarized this way: we affirm what Scripture affirms (a recent, literal creation week by a personal God), we hold what Scripture holds together (creation, Sabbath, marriage, fall, redemption), and we engage what Scripture invites us to explore (the wonders of the created world as evidence of divine wisdom). For further reading, search "creation" at egwwritings.org in Education, chapter 14.`,
