        id: "w2s-10-4-2",
        lessonId: "w2l-10-4",
        sectionType: "explain",
        title: "What Does This Mean?",
        content: `Romans 6:1-4 anticipates and demolishes the most dangerous misunderstanding of grace: that if salvation is free, sin no longer matters. Paul's response is not a polite correction but a visceral recoil \u2014 "Certainly not!" The logic is not merely moral but ontological: the believer has died to sin through union with Christ in baptism. A dead person does not return to the habits of their former life. Baptism is not a ceremonial accessory but a participation in Christ's death and resurrection; the old self was buried, and a new self was raised. To continue in deliberate sin is to behave as though the death and resurrection never happened.

James 2:14-26 addresses the opposite error: the belief that intellectual assent to correct doctrine constitutes saving faith. James is relentless in his logic \u2014 faith that produces no corresponding action is not merely inadequate; it is dead. His illustrations are pointed: Abraham's willingness to offer Isaac and Rahab's protection of the spies were not meritorious works that earned favor but visible evidence of a trust that was already alive within them. James does not contradict Paul; he confronts a different problem. Paul combats those who add works to faith as a condition of justification. James combats those who subtract works from faith as though obedience were optional.

Galatians 5:1,6 introduces a third misunderstanding: the return to legalism after experiencing grace. The Galatian churches were being pressured to adopt circumcision and Torah observance as requirements for full acceptance by God. Paul calls this a return to slavery. The liberty Christ purchased is not a license to sin (Romans 6) but a freedom from the futile attempt to earn God's favor through religious performance. The summary phrase \u2014 "faith working through love" \u2014 encapsulates the Pauline vision: genuine faith is active, but its energy source is love, not fear; its motive is gratitude, not merit.

These three passages together guard the biblical doctrine of salvation from its most persistent distortions: cheap grace that tolerates sin, dead orthodoxy that substitutes knowledge for trust, and enslaving legalism that replaces Christ's sufficiency with human effort.`,
