        id: "w2s-11-2-3",
        lessonId: "w2l-11-2",
        sectionType: "integrate",
        title: "SDA Theological Integration",
        content: `Adventist theology distinguishes carefully between justification and sanctification while insisting that both are works of grace received by faith. Justification is God's declaration that the repentant sinner is righteous on the basis of Christ's merit \u2014 it happens in a moment. Sanctification is God's ongoing work of making the justified believer actually righteous in character and conduct \u2014 it unfolds over a lifetime. Both are indispensable; neither replaces the other.

This distinction protects against two dangerous errors that have troubled the Christian church. The first is antinomianism \u2014 the idea that because we are justified by faith, obedience to God's law is irrelevant. The second is legalism \u2014 the belief that our obedience contributes to our justification. Adventists insist that the justified believer, indwelt by the Spirit, will progressively reflect God's character as expressed in His law \u2014 not to earn salvation but because the same grace that pardons also transforms.

The Adventist emphasis on holistic sanctification \u2014 spirit, soul, and body (1 Thessalonians 5:23) \u2014 connects directly to the church's health message, Sabbath observance, and stewardship teachings. These are not peripheral programs but expressions of a theology that takes seriously God's intention to restore the whole person. The body is not a prison to escape but a temple to honor (1 Corinthians 6:19-20). The Sabbath is not merely a day of rest but a weekly encounter with the sanctifying God (Exodus 31:13).

The phrase "from glory to glory" in 2 Corinthians 3:18 resonates with the Adventist understanding of progressive revelation and present truth. Just as the Spirit unfolds deeper understanding of Scripture across time, so the Spirit unfolds deeper Christlikeness within the individual believer. The process is real, measurable, and ongoing \u2014 but its completion awaits the return of Christ, when "we shall be like Him, for we shall see Him as He is" (1 John 3:2).`,
