        sectionType: "integrate",
        title: "SDA Theological Integration",
        content: `The three angels' messages form the theological backbone of Adventist identity and mission. They are not peripheral predictions for prophecy enthusiasts; they are the lens through which Adventists understand their reason for existing as a distinct movement.

**The everlasting gospel in judgment-hour context.** Adventists affirm that the pre-advent (investigative) judgment began in 1844, marking the transition described in Daniel 7:9-10 and the cleansing of the heavenly sanctuary in Daniel 8:14. This does not introduce a new gospel; it reveals a new phase of the gospel's application. The judgment is good news because it vindicates God's justice, confirms the reality of Christ's atonement, and demonstrates that heaven takes human decisions seriously. Adventists proclaim judgment not as a threat but as the assurance that God will set all things right.

**Sabbath as the seal of creation worship.** The first angel's call to worship the Creator directly invokes the Sabbath commandment. Adventists understand the seventh-day Sabbath as the enduring sign of allegiance to the God who made all things. In the final crisis, the Sabbath-versus-Sunday question becomes a visible test of loyalty \u2014 not because the day possesses magical qualities but because obedience to a clear command of God, when the world pressures compromise, reveals where a person's ultimate trust resides.

**"My people" in Babylon.** Revelation 18:4 is one of the most humbling texts in Adventist theology. God has faithful followers in every communion \u2014 people who love Him, serve Him, and follow the light they have received. The remnant's mission is to share additional light, not to pass judgment on those who have not yet encountered it. This recognition should produce generosity, not condescension. The remnant invites; it does not condemn.

**Corrective boundary.** This belief guards against two distortions: using the three angels' messages as a weapon of fear (sensationalist prophecy preaching that manipulates rather than informs) and diluting the messages until they lose their distinctive urgency (reducing Adventism to generic Christianity with a Sabbath add-on). The messages are urgent, specific, and compassionate \u2014 and they must be presented that way.`,
