        id: "w3s-13-3-2",
        lessonId: "w3l-13-3",
        sectionType: "explain",
        title: "What Does This Mean?",
        content: `The Great Commission in Matthew 28:18-20 is the church's standing orders. Jesus does not suggest mission as an optional activity for the spiritually ambitious; He commands it as the natural outworking of His authority. "All authority has been given to Me" \u2014 in heaven and on earth. Because Jesus reigns, His followers go. The mission is comprehensive: make disciples (not merely converts), baptize (incorporate into the covenant community), and teach (ongoing formation, not a one-time transaction). The scope is universal: "all the nations." And the assurance is permanent: "I am with you always, even to the end of the age." Mission is not something the church does in addition to its other activities; it is the reason the church exists.

Acts 1:8 adds a crucial element: the Holy Spirit as the source of missionary power. Jesus did not commission His followers and then leave them to their own resources. The Spirit provides the courage, wisdom, and supernatural enablement that human effort alone cannot produce. The geographic expansion \u2014 Jerusalem, Judea, Samaria, the end of the earth \u2014 models the principle of concentric outreach. Mission begins where you are and moves outward in ever-widening circles. No community is too near to be served; no nation is too far to be reached.

2 Peter 3:10-14 connects mission to eschatology. Peter's argument is striking: because the present world will be dissolved and a new creation is coming, believers should live in "holy conduct and godliness." The expectation of Christ's return does not produce withdrawal from the world; it produces intensified engagement. The phrase "hastening the coming of the day of God" suggests that the church's faithful witness actually participates in bringing about the consummation. Mission is not merely marking time until Jesus arrives; it is the means by which the gospel reaches every ear, after which the end will come (Matthew 24:14).

Together these texts establish that the remnant's mission is authorized by Christ, empowered by the Spirit, and motivated by the certainty of His return. It is not optional, not self-generated, and not limited by geography, culture, or human capacity.`,
