        title: "SDA Theological Integration",
        content: `The Adventist understanding of unity begins with a conviction that the church exists by divine initiative, not human decision. God calls, the Spirit baptizes, and Christ holds the body together. This sequence matters because it means unity is a gift to be received and maintained, not a project to be engineered.

**Unity grounded in truth, not sentiment.** Adventists affirm that genuine unity cannot be divorced from shared commitment to Scripture. Ephesians 4:13 places the goal as "the unity of the faith and of the knowledge of the Son of God." A unity that requires the abandonment of biblical convictions is not the unity Jesus prayed for \u2014 it is compromise wearing the language of love. At the same time, unity that elevates secondary opinions to the level of doctrinal essentials fractures the body over matters Scripture leaves open.

**Diversity as divine design.** The worldwide scope of the Adventist Church \u2014 present in over 200 countries \u2014 makes 1 Corinthians 12 more than theory. Worship styles, musical traditions, and cultural expressions vary enormously across the global body. The doctrine of unity affirms that this variety is not a concession but a reflection of a Creator who delights in diversity. The corrective boundary here guards against two errors: cultural imperialism (insisting one expression is normative for all) and cultural relativism (treating every practice as equally valid regardless of scriptural alignment).

**The Great Controversy and unity.** Within the cosmic conflict framework, division in the church serves the enemy's purposes. Satan's strategy has always been to fragment what God unites \u2014 beginning with the separation of humanity from God in Eden, continuing through ethnic hostility, class prejudice, and denominational rivalry. Every act of genuine reconciliation within the body of Christ is a demonstration that God's kingdom operates on different principles than the kingdoms of this world.

**Unity and mission.** Jesus tied the church's witness to its unity (John 17:21). Adventists understand that evangelistic effectiveness depends not only on the accuracy of the message but on the credibility of the messengers. A church that proclaims the three angels' messages while tolerating racial prejudice or economic elitism within its own ranks undermines the very proclamation it carries.`,
