        id: "w3s-14-2-3",
        lessonId: "w3l-14-2",
        sectionType: "integrate",
        title: "SDA Theological Integration",
        content: `The Adventist doctrinal formulation of unity draws on these texts to affirm several distinctive commitments:

**Unity is Trinitarian in origin.** The "seven ones" of Ephesians 4 trace unity from the one body (the church) through the one Spirit and one Lord to the one God and Father. Unity among believers mirrors and derives from the unity within the Godhead. This means the church's oneness is not merely an organizational convenience \u2014 it participates in the very life of God.

**Unity without uniformity.** Adventists operate in vastly different cultural contexts worldwide. The doctrine of unity affirms that fidelity to the same Lord, the same faith, and the same baptism does not require identical expressions of worship, governance detail, or social custom. The corrective boundary guards against two dangers: first, confusing cultural preference with doctrinal requirement (imposing one region's worship style on all); second, invoking "diversity" to justify departure from Scripture's clear teaching. Unity is anchored in truth; diversity operates within that anchor.

**The new humanity as eschatological sign.** Colossians 3's vision of a community where ethnic and social barriers have been dismantled is not an aspiration for a distant future \u2014 it is the present calling of the church. When a Sabbath congregation gathers people across racial, economic, and generational lines and treats every member with equal dignity, it enacts a preview of the new earth. This is the church functioning as an eschatological sign \u2014 a visible demonstration that God's kingdom has already broken into the present age.

**Creation-rooted solidarity.** Acts 17:26 provides a foundation for human rights and racial equality that predates the gospel: all humans share one origin. Adventists, who affirm a literal creation, have particular reason to insist on the biological and spiritual unity of the human family. The Sabbath itself \u2014 a memorial of creation \u2014 serves as a weekly reminder that every person bears the image of the same Creator.

**What this belief guards against.** The doctrine of unity guards against tribalism (defining the church by ethnicity, nationality, or political affiliation), elitism (creating tiers of membership based on education, wealth, or social status), and isolationism (refusing fellowship with believers who hold different secondary convictions).`,
