        content: "The practical outworking of unity touches every dimension of Adventist congregational life:\n\n**Every member a minister.** The Adventist understanding of spiritual gifts (rooted in Romans 12, 1 Corinthians 12, and Ephesians 4) insists that ministry is not the province of a professional class. Every baptized believer is a functioning member of the body with a contribution to make. Local churches thrive when they identify, affirm, and deploy the gifts of their members \u2014 and languish when ministry becomes the work of a few while the majority observe.\n\n**Unity in worship.** Adventist worship brings together diverse people under a common confession \u2014 the Sabbath itself is a great equalizer, gathering banker and farmer, professor and student, immigrant and native-born around the same Lord on the same day. The practical challenge is ensuring that worship styles honor both the tradition's heritage and the cultural realities of the local community without making either an idol.\n\n**Conflict resolution as discipleship.** Jesus provided a clear process for handling relational fracture within the church (Matthew 18:15-17). Adventist congregations honor unity not by pretending disagreements do not exist but by addressing them honestly, privately, and redemptively. Gossip, passive aggression, and silent withdrawal are all forms of division that undermine the body's health. The corrective boundary: unity does not mean the absence of disagreement; it means the presence of a commitment to work through disagreement within the framework of love and Scripture.\n\n**Cross-cultural fellowship as witness.** In a world increasingly fragmented along racial, political, and economic lines, a congregation that visibly practices mutual belonging across those lines is one of the most powerful apologetics available. The Adventist global family has a unique opportunity to demonstrate what Psalm 133 celebrates \u2014 the goodness and beauty of brothers and sisters dwelling together in genuine oneness.",
