        id: "w3s-14-4-2",
        lessonId: "w3l-14-4",
        sectionType: "explain",
        title: "What Does This Mean?",
        content: `The Corinthian church was not divided over doctrine \u2014 they were divided over personalities. "I am of Paul," "I am of Apollos," "I am of Cephas." The content of the gospel was not in dispute; loyalty to human leaders was. Paul's rhetorical question \u2014 "Is Christ divided?" \u2014 exposes the absurdity. The church belongs to Christ, not to any minister, movement, or faction. Personality-driven allegiance is one of the oldest and most persistent threats to Christian unity, and Paul treats it with undisguised alarm.

Philippians 2:1-4 provides the internal remedy: a fundamental reorientation of the self. "Let each esteem others better than himself" is not false modesty \u2014 it is a disciplined refusal to place personal preference, reputation, or comfort above the well-being of the community. Paul grounds this exhortation in Christ's own example (the verses that follow describe the kenosis \u2014 Christ's self-emptying). Unity is sustained not by rules but by the cultivation of a Christlike disposition that considers others first.

2 Corinthians 5:16-17 addresses a subtler threat: the tendency to evaluate people by external categories. "We regard no one according to the flesh" means that ethnic heritage, social standing, educational achievement, and political alignment no longer determine how believers see one another. The "new creation" Paul announces is not merely an individual experience \u2014 it generates a new way of perceiving every other person. Old classifications dissolve, not because differences cease to exist, but because they cease to define.

Taken together, these passages identify three persistent threats to unity \u2014 faction loyalty, selfish ambition, and identity defined by worldly categories \u2014 and prescribe a single remedy: radical reorientation around Christ. The church that takes these texts seriously will resist the temptation to organize itself around charismatic leaders, cultural tribes, or political alliances and will continually return to the one Lord who holds it together.`,
