        id: "w3s-15-1-3",
        lessonId: "w3l-15-1",
        sectionType: "integrate",
        title: "SDA Theological Integration",
        content: "The Adventist understanding of baptism is shaped by these foundational texts in several distinctive ways:\n\n**Baptism as participation, not mere illustration.** While Adventists do not hold a sacramental view (the water itself does not confer grace), they take Paul's participatory language seriously. Baptism is more than a visual aid \u2014 it is the enacted, public moment when the believer's identification with Christ's death and resurrection becomes concrete. The candidate is saying with their body what faith says with the heart: I have died to my old life; I am alive in Christ.\n\n**The inseparability of repentance and baptism.** Acts 2:38 establishes a sequence that Adventists regard as normative: repentance precedes baptism. This conviction underlies the practice of baptizing only those who have made a conscious, informed decision to follow Christ. Baptism is a response to the gospel, not a precondition for hearing it. This is why Adventist churches typically offer baptismal classes \u2014 not to create bureaucratic hurdles, but to ensure that candidates understand the covenant they are entering.\n\n**The Trinitarian context.** Matthew 28:19 grounds baptism in the name of the triune God. This is not a formula to be recited mechanically; it is a declaration that the baptized person now belongs to Father, Son, and Spirit. The Adventist practice of asking candidates to affirm their faith in each Person of the Godhead before entering the water reflects this Trinitarian structure.\n\n**Baptism and the local church.** Adventists affirm that baptism is a church ordinance \u2014 it is properly administered within the context of a believing community, not as a private transaction. The local congregation receives the baptized person into fellowship, and the community commits to nurturing, teaching, and supporting the new believer. This communal dimension is essential; the body metaphor of 1 Corinthians 12 means that a new member changes the composition of the entire organism.\n\n**What this belief guards against.** The doctrine of baptism guards against two errors: first, the reduction of baptism to a meaningless tradition performed without understanding or commitment; second, the elevation of baptism to a saving work that replaces faith. Baptism is a response of faith, empowered by grace, enacted in community.",
