        id: "w3s-15-2-2",
        lessonId: "w3l-15-2",
        sectionType: "explain",
        title: "What Does This Mean?",
        content: `Colossians 2:12-13 reaffirms and deepens the theology of Romans 6. The believer is "buried with Him in baptism" and "raised with Him through faith in the working of God." Two details deserve attention. First, the raising is attributed to faith \u2014 not to the water itself, but to the believer's trust in the God who raised Jesus from the dead. Baptism without faith is empty ritual; faith without baptism lacks the embodied confession God has prescribed. Second, Paul adds the dimension of forgiveness: God "has made alive together with Him, having forgiven you all trespasses." The new life that begins at baptism is not merely a fresh start in the moral sense \u2014 it is a life cleansed, pardoned, and reconciled to God.

Matthew 3:13-17 records the baptism of Jesus Himself \u2014 the most theologically puzzling baptism in history. Jesus had no sins to confess, no old self to bury. John recognized the incongruity: "I need to be baptized by You." Jesus' answer \u2014 "it is fitting for us to fulfill all righteousness" \u2014 suggests that His baptism served a different purpose. He was identifying with the humanity He came to save, entering the water as the representative of a fallen race, consecrating the rite by His own participation. And the Father's response \u2014 the opened heavens, the descending Spirit, the audible declaration of pleasure \u2014 transformed the Jordan into a throne room. Every subsequent baptism carries this echo: the triune God is present, the candidate is received, and heaven approves.

Acts 22:16 captures the urgency. Ananias does not invite Paul to consider baptism at his leisure; he says, "Why are you waiting?" The verb tense suggests immediacy \u2014 the decisive step should not be delayed once the heart has been converted. Baptism is not a graduation ceremony scheduled after years of study; it is the first public act of a life surrendered to Christ. The washing imagery ("wash away your sins") does not attribute magical properties to water but acknowledges that baptism is the divinely appointed expression of the cleansing already accomplished by grace.`,
