        title: "What Does This Mean?",
        content: `1 Peter 3:21 is one of the most carefully balanced statements in the New Testament on the subject of baptism. Peter says baptism "saves us" \u2014 a startling claim that could easily be misread. But he immediately adds a parenthetical clarification: "not the removal of the filth of the flesh, but the answer of a good conscience toward God." The water does not save by washing the body; baptism saves as the expression of a conscience that has responded to God's call. And even this saving power is grounded not in the rite itself but "through the resurrection of Jesus Christ." Peter holds three realities in tension: baptism matters, it is not magic, and its power comes from Christ's resurrection.

Ephesians 4:5 \u2014 "one baptism" \u2014 raises an important question: if there is only one baptism, is rebaptism ever appropriate? The phrase likely refers to the singularity of baptism as a Christian ordinance (distinguishing it from the multiple ritual washings of Judaism). It does not necessarily prohibit rebaptism in every circumstance. The New Testament itself records a case where disciples of John the Baptist, who had received a preparatory baptism but not yet encountered the full gospel, were baptized again in the name of Jesus (Acts 19:1-5). The principle emerges: rebaptism is appropriate when the original baptism did not reflect genuine understanding and commitment, not as a casual repetition but as a considered response to deeper conversion.

Mark 16:16 pairs belief and baptism in the affirmative ("He who believes and is baptized will be saved") but mentions only belief in the negative ("he who does not believe will be condemned"). This asymmetry is significant. Salvation is conditioned on faith; baptism is the expected and commanded expression of that faith. The verse does not teach that an unbaptized believer (such as the thief on the cross) is lost \u2014 but it does make clear that deliberate refusal of baptism, when one has the opportunity, is inconsistent with genuine faith. Baptism is not optional for those who can obey; it is the normal path of discipleship.`,
