        title: "SDA Theological Integration",
        content: `Seventh-day Adventists read the biblical testimony about prophecy with two convictions held in careful balance: the prophetic gift did not expire with the apostolic age, and no prophetic voice ever rivals or replaces the authority of Scripture.

**Continuity, not cessation.** Paul lists prophecy among the gifts the ascended Christ gives to His church "till we all come to the unity of the faith and of the knowledge of the Son of God, to a perfect man" (Ephesians 4:11-13). Since that unity has not yet been achieved, the gifts \u2014 including prophecy \u2014 remain operative. Joel's promise, endorsed by Peter, points to an intensification of prophetic activity in the last days, not its withdrawal. Adventists affirm that the same God who spoke through Deborah, Isaiah, and Agabus continues to speak through the prophetic gift whenever and wherever He chooses.

**Scripture as supreme authority.** The relationship between prophetic counsel and the Bible is not one of equality but of subordination. Isaiah 8:20 sets the standard: "To the law and to the testimony! If they do not speak according to this word, it is because there is no light in them." Any prophetic claim that contradicts, supplements, or functionally replaces the Bible fails the most basic biblical test. Adventists affirm that the sixty-six books of Scripture constitute the closed canon \u2014 the authoritative, sufficient, and final standard of faith and practice. Prophetic ministry after the canon functions as a guiding light that points back to Scripture, never beyond it.

**The prophetic gift and the remnant.** Revelation 12:17 identifies two marks of the end-time remnant: keeping the commandments of God and holding to the testimony of Jesus. Revelation 19:10 defines the testimony of Jesus as "the spirit of prophecy." Adventists understand this connection to mean that the prophetic gift is an identifying characteristic of God's last-day people \u2014 not a badge of superiority, but a provision of grace for a community navigating the final chapters of the great controversy.

**Corrective boundary.** This belief guards against two opposite errors. On one side stands cessationism \u2014 the claim that God stopped giving prophetic gifts after the apostolic era, a position that lacks explicit biblical support and contradicts Joel's eschatological promise. On the other side stands uncritical credulity \u2014 the willingness to accept every prophetic claim without testing it against Scripture. Adventists reject both extremes, insisting on open-handed expectation of the Spirit's work combined with rigorous biblical evaluation of every claim made in God's name.`,
