        lessonId: "w4l-19-2",
        sectionType: "integrate",
        title: "SDA Theological Integration",
        content: "Adventist theology affirms an inseparable connection between grace and law. The two are not competing forces pulling in opposite directions; they are complementary aspects of God's redemptive work. Grace saves, and the law defines the shape of the saved life.\n\n**Justification and sanctification distinguished but never separated.** Adventists teach that justification (being declared righteous through faith in Christ) is complete and immediate \u2014 Romans 8:1 stands without qualification. Sanctification (being made righteous through the Spirit's transforming work) is progressive and lifelong \u2014 Romans 8:4 describes an ongoing walk. The law cannot justify anyone; only Christ's righteousness received by faith accomplishes that. But the law reveals the character of God and therefore serves as the pattern of sanctified living. To reject the law after accepting grace is like accepting a prescription for health but refusing to follow it.\n\n**The new covenant is not a no-law covenant.** Hebrews 8:10 explicitly states that the new covenant involves God's laws being written on the heart. Some interpreters claim that the new covenant replaces the law with the Spirit, as though the two are mutually exclusive. Adventists reject this reading. The Spirit is the agent who writes the law on the heart; the law is the content the Spirit inscribes. They work together, not in opposition.\n\n**Ephesians 2:8-10 as the complete picture.** Adventists often emphasize that quoting verses 8-9 without verse 10 produces a truncated gospel. Grace that saves but does not transform is not the grace Paul describes. Equally, works without grace as their source are self-righteous performance. The full passage holds both truths in creative tension: radical dependence on grace for salvation and wholehearted engagement in the good works God has prepared.\n\n**Corrective boundary.** This formulation guards against cheap grace \u2014 the idea that because salvation is free, how we live does not matter. It equally guards against works-righteousness \u2014 the idea that our behavior secures our standing before God. The biblical position is grace-secured standing expressed through Spirit-empowered obedience.",
