        sectionType: "integrate",
        title: "SDA Theological Integration",
        content: `Adventists emphasize that genuine commandment-keeping is always the fruit of a living relationship with Christ, never a substitute for it. The law describes the shape of a life animated by God's indwelling Spirit.

**Walking as He walked.** The Adventist understanding of Christian living takes 1 John 2:6 seriously: the believer's daily walk should reflect the character and priorities of Jesus. This encompasses not only the Ten Commandments but also Christ's attitudes \u2014 humility, compassion, truthfulness, justice, and mercy. Obedience is not a checklist of prohibitions; it is a living portrait of Christ's character reproduced in human experience through the Holy Spirit's power.

**Love as the motive, law as the standard.** Matthew 22:37-40 demonstrates that love and law are not competitors. Love is the motive; the commandments define the standard. Without love, commandment-keeping degenerates into cold compliance. Without the commandments, love becomes sentimental and undefined. The Adventist position holds both together: "We love because He first loved us" (1 John 4:19), and that love expresses itself in concrete obedience to God's revealed will.

**The end-time witness.** Revelation 14:12 describes those who endure the final crisis as people who "keep the commandments of God and the faith of Jesus." Adventists understand this as a community that has internalized God's law through the Spirit's work (Hebrews 8:10) and lives in dependence on Christ's righteousness. The combination is significant: commandment-keeping without faith becomes self-righteousness; faith without commandment-keeping becomes presumption. God's final people hold both in living balance.

**Corrective boundary.** This teaching guards against the false dichotomy between love and law. When someone claims that "love is all that matters" and uses that claim to dismiss specific commandments, they are setting human definitions of love above God's revealed standard. Equally, when someone insists on obedience while neglecting mercy, compassion, and humility, they replicate the error Jesus addressed in the Pharisees (Matthew 23:23). The biblical pattern is love expressed through obedience, obedience motivated by love.`,
