        sectionType: "integrate",
        title: "SDA Theological Integration",
        content: `Adventists encounter persistent objections to their understanding of God's law. Three misunderstandings require careful, biblical responses.

**"Paul abolished the law."** This reading of Galatians and Romans fails to distinguish between the ceremonial law (the sacrificial system that pointed forward to Christ and was fulfilled at the cross \u2014 Colossians 2:14-17) and the moral law (the Ten Commandments, which reflect God's eternal character). Paul's polemic in Galatians targets the demand that Gentile converts be circumcised and observe ceremonial regulations as a condition of salvation. He never argues against the moral law. In Romans 3:31, he explicitly states: "Do we then make void the law through faith? Certainly not! On the contrary, we establish the law." Adventists maintain this distinction not as a theological convenience but as a reading demanded by the texts themselves.

**"Any emphasis on obedience is legalism."** This confuses the motive of obedience with the fact of obedience. Legalism is the attempt to earn God's favor through one's own moral performance. Grace-motivated obedience is the grateful response of a heart already assured of God's favor through Christ. The same Paul who wrote "by grace you have been saved through faith" also wrote "the righteous requirement of the law might be fulfilled in us" (Romans 8:4). Rejecting obedience in the name of grace is not Pauline theology; it is antinomianism \u2014 a heresy the apostle explicitly combated.

**"Christians are only under the law of love, not the Ten Commandments."** This creates a false separation between love and the Decalogue. Jesus Himself said that the entire law hangs on love for God and love for neighbor (Matthew 22:40). The Ten Commandments spell out what that love looks like in specific situations. "Love your neighbor" is a beautiful principle; "You shall not steal" tells you what love looks like when your neighbor has something you want. The commandments are love made concrete.

**Corrective boundary.** This teaching guards against the selective reading of Paul that extracts his statements about freedom from their context and uses them to dismiss moral accountability. It equally guards against using obedience as a measuring stick for judging others \u2014 a pattern Jesus condemned in the Pharisees (Luke 18:9-14).`,
