        id: "w4s-20-3-3",
        lessonId: "w4l-20-3",
        sectionType: "integrate",
        title: "SDA Theological Integration",
        content: `Adventist Sabbath practice aims to recover the delight, community, and worship that Scripture associates with the seventh day.

**Delight, not deprivation.** Isaiah 58:13-14 calls believers to experience the Sabbath as a delight. Adventist Sabbath observance, at its best, reflects this \u2014 Sabbath is characterized by worship, fellowship, nature walks, family time, acts of kindness, and spiritual renewal. The day is meant to be the highlight of the week, not an endurance test. When Sabbath-keeping becomes a list of prohibitions ("don't do this, don't do that"), it has drifted from the prophetic vision. The question is not merely "What should I avoid on Sabbath?" but "What makes Sabbath delightful?"

**Corporate worship as custom.** Luke 4:16 establishes Jesus's pattern of regular synagogue attendance on the Sabbath. Adventists follow this example through weekly Sabbath School (Bible study) and worship services. The communal dimension of the Sabbath is not optional; it is central. Rest in isolation is incomplete rest \u2014 the Sabbath is designed for shared worship, shared learning, and shared meals. The Adventist tradition of potluck fellowship meals after Sabbath worship embodies this communal vision.

**Sabbath as mission.** Isaiah 56:6-7 opens the Sabbath to all nations. Adventists understand this as a mandate for inclusion: the Sabbath is not a boundary that excludes outsiders but a gift that invites them. Sharing the Sabbath experience with neighbors, friends, and seekers is an act of hospitality, not proselytizing. The Sabbath itself, when experienced in its fullness \u2014 rest, worship, community, nature, joy \u2014 becomes a compelling witness.

**Corrective boundary.** This teaching guards against Sabbath legalism \u2014 the tendency to define the day by what is prohibited rather than by what is celebrated. When "keeping the Sabbath" becomes primarily about avoiding activities, the prophetic vision of delight has been lost. It equally guards against Sabbath casualness \u2014 treating the day as merely a personal preference rather than a sacred appointment with the Creator.`,
