        id: "w4s-20-4-2",
        lessonId: "w4l-20-4",
        sectionType: "explain",
        title: "What Does This Mean?",
        content: `Colossians 2:16-17 is the passage most frequently cited against Sabbath observance. Paul tells the Colossians not to let anyone judge them regarding "food or drink, or regarding a festival or a new moon or sabbaths." The critical question is: which sabbaths? The sequence Paul lists \u2014 festivals, new moons, sabbaths \u2014 mirrors the pattern found in Old Testament ceremonial texts (1 Chronicles 23:31; 2 Chronicles 2:4; 2 Chronicles 31:3; Ezekiel 45:17). In each of these passages, the three terms refer to the annual, monthly, and ceremonial sabbath observances connected to Israel's festival calendar \u2014 not the weekly seventh-day Sabbath established at creation. Paul calls these observances "a shadow of things to come, but the substance is of Christ." The ceremonial calendar \u2014 with its Passover, Day of Atonement, and sabbatical years \u2014 pointed forward to Christ and found its fulfillment in Him. The weekly Sabbath, by contrast, points backward to creation and is grounded in God's character as Creator, not in the typological system.

Romans 14:5-6 addresses disputes about days within the Roman church. The context (vv. 1-4) involves "doubtful things" \u2014 matters of personal conviction, not moral absolutes. Paul mentions food (vegetarianism vs. eating meat) and days (esteeming one day above another). The most natural reading, given the mixed Jewish-Gentile audience, is that the disputed "days" are fast days or festival observances \u2014 not the weekly Sabbath. Paul never lists the Sabbath among debatable matters elsewhere in his writings, and his consistent synagogue attendance on the Sabbath (Acts 13:14, 42, 44; 16:13; 17:2; 18:4) demonstrates his own practice.

Acts 20:7 records a gathering on "the first day of the week" at which Paul spoke and broke bread. Some argue this establishes Sunday as the new Christian day of worship. However, several factors undermine this reading. First, the gathering occurred in the evening \u2014 by biblical reckoning, the first day of the week began at Saturday sunset, making this a Saturday evening meeting. Second, Paul planned to depart the next morning on foot (v. 13), suggesting this was a farewell gathering, not a regular worship pattern. Third, "breaking bread" was a daily practice in the early church (Acts 2:46), not a distinctive act reserved for a specific day. A single farewell meeting does not constitute a change in the day of worship.`,
