        sectionType: "integrate",
        title: "SDA Theological Integration",
        content: `Adventists address these passages with careful exegetical reasoning, not dismissiveness. Each text deserves honest engagement.

**Colossians 2:16-17 and the ceremonial distinction.** Adventists maintain that Paul's reference to "sabbaths" in Colossians 2:16 points to the ceremonial sabbaths of the Levitical calendar, not the weekly Sabbath of the Decalogue. The evidence is contextual: the list matches Old Testament festival sequences, and Paul describes these as "a shadow of things to come" \u2014 language appropriate for the typological ceremonial system but not for the creation-rooted weekly Sabbath. The seventh-day Sabbath is never called a shadow in Scripture; it is called a sign (Ezekiel 20:12), a memorial (Exodus 20:8-11), and a gift (Mark 2:27).

**Romans 14 and matters of conscience.** Adventists note that Romans 14 deals with adiaphora \u2014 matters where Scripture does not give an absolute command and individual conscience governs. The Sabbath commandment is not in this category; it is one of the Ten Commandments, spoken by God, written by His finger, and placed in the ark of the covenant. Paul would not classify a Decalogue commandment as a matter of personal preference. The disputed "days" are most naturally understood as Jewish fast days or festival observances that Gentile converts were uncertain about.

**Acts 20:7 and the question of Sunday.** The New Testament contains no command to transfer Sabbath sanctity to the first day of the week. Acts 20:7 records one evening gathering; it does not establish a weekly pattern. The shift from Sabbath to Sunday worship occurred gradually in the centuries following the apostolic era, driven by anti-Jewish sentiment, Roman imperial influence, and ecclesiastical decisions \u2014 not by apostolic command. Historical honesty requires acknowledging that the change was a post-biblical development, not a New Testament mandate.

**Corrective boundary.** This teaching guards against two opposite errors: an adversarial posture that treats Sunday-keeping Christians as enemies of truth (the Sabbath should be presented as a gift, not a weapon) and a capitulation that treats the day of worship as spiritually insignificant (when Scripture specifies the seventh day and provides no authorization to change it).`,
