        title: "SDA Theological Integration",
        content: "Several persistent misunderstandings surround stewardship, and Adventist theology addresses them with both conviction and pastoral care:\n\n**Misunderstanding 1: Stewardship equals tithing.** Many reduce stewardship to a ten-percent calculation. While tithe is an important component, Jesus' rebuke in Matthew 23:23 makes clear that financial faithfulness without justice, mercy, and faith is incomplete. A person who tithes meticulously but hoards time, neglects health, or exploits relationships has not grasped whole-life stewardship.\n\n**Misunderstanding 2: Prosperity theology.** Some teach that generous giving triggers automatic material return \u2014 a kind of spiritual vending machine. Malachi 3:10 is often cited in support, but the promise of blessing must be read within the full biblical witness, which includes faithful people who experienced poverty, persecution, and loss. God blesses faithfulness, but blessing takes many forms \u2014 and the ultimate reward is eternal, not temporal.\n\n**Misunderstanding 3: Stewardship is only about money.** Even within church communities, stewardship campaigns often focus narrowly on financial giving. The biblical vision is far broader: time, talent, health, influence, creation care, and relational integrity are all stewardship domains. An effective stewardship theology addresses the whole person, not just the wallet.\n\n**Misunderstanding 4: Saving and investing are unspiritual.** Jesus condemned hoarding, not prudent planning. Proverbs commends the ant for storing in summer (Proverbs 6:6-8). The steward who buried his talent was rebuked not for caution but for faithlessness (Matthew 25:24-27). Financial wisdom \u2014 saving for the future, avoiding unnecessary debt, investing thoughtfully \u2014 is part of responsible stewardship, not a contradiction of generosity.\n\n**Corrective boundary:** Stewardship appeals must never manipulate through guilt, shame, or spiritual threats. God invites cheerful giving (2 Corinthians 9:7). Any approach that coerces, pressures, or weaponizes Scripture to extract resources violates the spirit of the very doctrine it claims to uphold.",
