        id: "w4s-23-4-2",
        lessonId: "w4l-23-4",
        sectionType: "explain",
        title: "Navigating Difficult Questions",
        content: `Marriage is God's ideal, but we live in a fallen world where that ideal is frequently damaged. Scripture addresses this reality with both honesty and compassion. Jesus taught that divorce was not part of God's original plan \u2014 "from the beginning it was not so" (Matthew 19:8) \u2014 yet He acknowledged that Moses permitted it because of the hardness of human hearts. Paul, writing to the Corinthians, relays the Lord's command that a wife should not separate from her husband and a husband should not divorce his wife (1 Corinthians 7:10-11). Yet he also addresses situations where an unbelieving spouse chooses to leave: "if the unbeliever departs, let him depart; a brother or a sister is not under bondage in such cases" (7:15). Scripture does not ignore the pain of broken marriages; it meets that pain with pastoral wisdom.

Two common misunderstandings need correction. First, the idea that divorced persons are permanently disqualified from God's grace or church fellowship distorts the gospel. While divorce grieves God's heart (Malachi 2:16), it does not place anyone beyond the reach of forgiveness and restoration. Second, the assumption that singleness is a deficiency contradicts Paul's teaching. Paul described his own singleness as a gift (1 Corinthians 7:7) and commended it as a state that allows undivided devotion to the Lord (7:35). The church must be a place where single adults, divorced persons, widows, and blended families all find belonging, dignity, and support \u2014 not judgment or exclusion.`,
