        content: "The Adventist Church seeks to hold together two convictions that are sometimes perceived as conflicting: the high ideal of lifelong marriage and the pastoral compassion required when that ideal is broken. Both are rooted in Scripture.\n\nKey pastoral principles include:\n\n1. **Upholding the ideal without condemning the broken** \u2014 Adventists affirm that marriage is intended to be a lifelong covenant. At the same time, the church recognizes that sin damages relationships, and that Scripture itself makes provision for situations where marriages end (Matthew 19:9; 1 Corinthians 7:15). The church's role is not to impose shame but to offer grace, guidance, and community support.\n2. **Remarriage with pastoral discernment** \u2014 The Adventist Church Manual provides guidelines for situations involving remarriage, seeking to balance fidelity to Scripture with sensitivity to individual circumstances. Pastoral counsel and congregational care are essential in these situations.\n3. **Valuing singleness** \u2014 The church must resist the cultural assumption that marriage is the only path to a fulfilled life. Paul's commendation of singleness (1 Corinthians 7:7-8,32-35) reminds us that wholeness is found in Christ, not in marital status. Single members deserve full inclusion, respect, and meaningful ministry roles.\n4. **Restoration as the goal** \u2014 Malachi 4:5-6 promises that God will turn hearts toward reconciliation. The church's ministry to families should always point toward healing, whether that means restoring a strained marriage, supporting a grieving divorcee, or helping a blended family build new foundations.\n\n**Corrective boundary:** This belief guards against both permissiveness (treating marriage as disposable) and rigidity (using biblical standards as weapons against wounded people). The gospel holds truth and grace together.\n\nFor further reading, visit adventist.org/marriage-and-the-family.",
