        id: "w4s-24-2-2",
        lessonId: "w4l-24-2",
        sectionType: "explain",
        title: "Christ's Two-Phase Ministry",
        content: `The earthly sanctuary operated on two cycles: a daily ministry of intercession and a yearly Day of Atonement. Each day, sacrifices were offered and blood was brought into the Holy Place, symbolizing the continual availability of forgiveness and access to God. Once a year, on the Day of Atonement (Yom Kippur), the high priest entered the Most Holy Place to cleanse the sanctuary of the accumulated record of confessed sins (Leviticus 16). This annual event represented the ultimate resolution of the sin problem \u2014 not just forgiveness, but the removal of sin's record from God's dwelling.

Adventists understand Christ's heavenly ministry as following this two-phase pattern. After His ascension, Christ began His intercessory ministry in the heavenly sanctuary, applying the benefits of His sacrifice to all who come to God through Him (Hebrews 7:25). Daniel 7:9-14 describes a judgment scene that takes place before the second coming: the Ancient of Days is seated, the books are opened, and the Son of Man is brought before the Father to receive dominion and a kingdom. Adventists identify this pre-advent judgment as the antitype (the heavenly fulfillment) of the Day of Atonement. Daniel 8:14 provides the prophetic time marker: at the conclusion of the 2,300-day prophecy, "the sanctuary shall be cleansed." Using the day-year principle (Numbers 14:34; Ezekiel 4:6), Adventists calculate this period as beginning in 457 BC (the decree to restore Jerusalem, Daniel 9:25) and concluding in 1844 AD, when Christ's ministry entered its final phase of judgment and vindication.`,
