        title: "SDA Theological Integration",
        content: `Seventh-day Adventists hold the Second Coming of Christ at the very center of their identity. The word "Adventist" itself declares this conviction \u2014 they are a people defined by the advent, the coming of Christ.

**Hope, not speculation.** The Adventist emphasis on the Second Coming is rooted in Scripture's repeated declarations, not in prophetic calculation. While the movement's history includes the painful disappointment of 1844, the enduring legacy of that experience was not a retreat from hope but a deepened commitment to letting Scripture \u2014 rather than human timetables \u2014 define the shape of expectation. Jesus said plainly, "Of that day and hour no one knows, not even the angels of heaven, but My Father only" (Matthew 24:36). Adventists take this declaration seriously. The appropriate posture is watchful readiness, not calendar-based prediction.

**The personal return of a personal Savior.** Adventist theology insists that the Second Coming is literal, visible, audible, and personal. Christ does not return as a force, an influence, or a philosophical ideal. He returns as the same Jesus who lived in Galilee, died on Calvary, and rose from the tomb. Acts 1:9-11 provides the definitive interpretive framework: "in like manner." This guards the church against both over-spiritualized interpretations that strip the event of its concreteness and overly politicized expectations that reduce it to earthly power dynamics.

**The grand climax of the gospel.** For Adventists, the Second Coming is not an isolated end-time event but the culmination of the entire plan of salvation. Everything the gospel promises \u2014 justification, sanctification, glorification, the restoration of all things \u2014 reaches its completion when Christ appears. The cross provides the basis; the resurrection provides the guarantee; the Second Coming provides the consummation. This is why Paul calls it "the blessed hope" and why Adventist worship, mission, and daily living are oriented toward this event.

**Corrective boundary.** This doctrine guards against two errors: hopeless resignation that assumes the world's brokenness is permanent, and triumphalist activism that believes human effort alone will usher in God's kingdom. The Second Coming declares that God Himself will intervene decisively \u2014 and that this intervention is certain, personal, and imminent in the biblical sense of "always the next great event."`,
