        id: "w4s-26-4-3",
        lessonId: "w4l-26-4",
        sectionType: "integrate",
        title: "SDA Theological Integration",
        content: `Seventh-day Adventists approach the commonly cited "problem passages" about death with careful exegesis and pastoral sensitivity. The goal is not to win arguments but to let Scripture interpret Scripture \u2014 allowing the clearest passages to illuminate the more ambiguous ones.

**The principle of scriptural consistency.** The Bible contains hundreds of passages that describe death as sleep, unconsciousness, and the cessation of thought (Ecclesiastes 9:5-6, Psalm 146:3-4, Psalm 115:17, Daniel 12:2, John 11:11-14). Against this overwhelming testimony, a handful of passages \u2014 most of them figurative, poetic, or parabolic \u2014 are sometimes read as teaching conscious existence after death. Sound hermeneutics requires interpreting the few in light of the many, the ambiguous in light of the clear, and the figurative in light of the literal. Adventists do not dismiss the difficult passages; they read them in context and within the framework of Scripture's broader testimony.

**The rich man and Lazarus.** Adventists recognize this as a parable \u2014 part of a series in Luke 15-16 \u2014 whose purpose is to challenge the wealthy religious leaders, not to map the geography of the afterlife. Jesus uses familiar cultural imagery (Abraham's bosom, Hades) to deliver a moral lesson about compassion, justice, and the sufficiency of Scripture ("Moses and the prophets"). Reading the parable as literal theology creates contradictions with the rest of Scripture and distorts Jesus' intended message.

**The thief on the cross.** The alternative punctuation of Luke 23:43 \u2014 "I say to you today, you will be with Me in Paradise" \u2014 is grammatically valid and consistent with the broader biblical witness. Jesus did not ascend to paradise on Friday (John 20:17). The promise to the thief is about the certainty of salvation, not the timing of consciousness. The thief will indeed be with Christ in paradise \u2014 at the resurrection.

**Paul's desire in Philippians.** Paul's longing to "depart and be with Christ" reflects the subjective experience of the believer for whom death eliminates the consciousness of time. The next thing Paul will experience after death is the voice of Christ at the resurrection. From his perspective, death and the presence of Christ are subjectively immediate \u2014 even though objectively, centuries may pass.

**Corrective boundary.** This careful exegesis guards against two errors: building doctrine on figurative language alone, and dismissing difficult passages without engaging them honestly. Adventists take every text seriously \u2014 but they let clear texts govern the interpretation of unclear ones, following the Reformation principle of sola Scriptura.`,
