        title: "What Does This Mean?",
        content: `Revelation 20 introduces a period of one thousand years that serves as a bridge between two decisive events: the second coming of Christ and the final eradication of sin. The chapter opens with an angel binding Satan and casting him into a "bottomless pit" \u2014 the Greek word abyssos, a term that in the Septuagint describes the formless, empty earth before creation (Genesis 1:2). Satan's binding is not merely physical restraint; it is the removal of his audience. With the righteous taken to heaven and the wicked destroyed by the brightness of Christ's coming (2 Thessalonians 2:8), there is no one left for Satan to deceive. He is confined to a depopulated, ruined planet \u2014 a prisoner of circumstance as much as of divine decree.

The vision then shifts to thrones and judgment. Those who "lived and reigned with Christ" for a thousand years are the resurrected righteous \u2014 participants in a review of God's judicial decisions. This is not a trial to determine guilt or innocence; that determination was settled before Christ's return. It is an open audit of God's justice, an opportunity for the redeemed to examine the records, understand why certain individuals are absent, and confirm that every decision God made was fair, merciful, and transparent. Paul anticipated this role when he wrote to the Corinthians that the saints would judge the world and even angels (1 Corinthians 6:2-3). The millennium is the time when that promise finds its fulfillment.

Two resurrections frame the millennium. The "first resurrection" at its beginning raises the righteous dead to join the living saints who are transformed at Christ's coming (1 Thessalonians 4:16-17). The rest of the dead \u2014 the unrighteous \u2014 "did not live again until the thousand years were finished" (Revelation 20:5). This separation is critical: the millennium is bounded by two distinct resurrections, not one general rising of all the dead. The sequence anchors the entire doctrine in a chronological framework that Revelation presents with striking clarity.`,
