        content: `The Adventist understanding of the millennium organizes the biblical data into three distinct phases, each marked by specific events that Scripture describes with remarkable precision.

**Phase One: The Beginning of the Millennium.** Christ returns visibly, gloriously, and personally. The righteous dead are raised in the first resurrection. The living righteous are transformed (1 Corinthians 15:51-52). Together, they are caught up to meet the Lord in the air (1 Thessalonians 4:16-17) and are taken to the heavenly dwelling places Christ prepared (John 14:1-3). The wicked who are alive at His coming are destroyed by the brightness of His appearing (2 Thessalonians 2:8). Satan is bound \u2014 not by literal chains, but by the chain of circumstances: there is no one left on earth to tempt. The earth lies desolate, returning to a state that Jeremiah describes using the same Hebrew phrase \u2014 tohu vavohu, "without form and void" \u2014 that Genesis 1:2 uses for the primordial chaos.

**Phase Two: During the Millennium.** The redeemed are in heaven, seated on thrones, engaged in the work of judgment (Revelation 20:4). This is the review phase \u2014 the saints examine the records of God's dealings with every case and confirm the justice of His decisions. Meanwhile, on the ruined earth, Satan and his angels have nothing to do but contemplate the results of their rebellion. There is no second-chance ministry, no earthly kingdom, no ongoing human civilization. The earth is a silent witness to what sin produces when it runs its full course.

**Phase Three: The Close of the Millennium.** The Holy City, the New Jerusalem, descends from heaven to earth (Revelation 21:2). The wicked dead are raised in the second resurrection (Revelation 20:5). Satan, now released from his enforced inactivity, rallies the resurrected wicked for one final assault on the City of God (Revelation 20:7-9). Fire comes down from God and devours them (Revelation 20:9). Ezekiel 28:18-19 describes this end in language applied to the power behind the king of Tyre \u2014 a being who is consumed from within, reduced to ashes, and ceases to exist forever. Sin, sinners, and Satan are no more. The universe is clean.`,
