        title: "SDA Theological Integration",
        content: `Seventh-day Adventists hold that the three-phase structure of the millennium is not a speculative scheme imposed on Scripture but a reading that follows the sequence Revelation 20 itself presents.

**Two resurrections, not one.** The distinction between the first and second resurrections is foundational. Many Christian traditions read a single, general resurrection at the end of history. Revelation 20:5-6, however, explicitly states that "the rest of the dead did not live again until the thousand years were finished" and pronounces a blessing on those who share in the first resurrection. This two-resurrection framework gives the millennium its chronological boundaries and its theological meaning. The first resurrection vindicates the righteous; the second resurrection precedes the final judgment of the wicked.

**The saints' role in judgment.** The millennial review is not redundant. It serves the universe. God's character has been under assault since Lucifer first raised questions about divine governance. The millennium provides a forum where every created being can examine the evidence and arrive at an informed verdict. When the redeemed declare God's ways "just and true" (Revelation 15:3), they do so not because they were told to but because they have seen the records for themselves. This transparency is the foundation on which the eternal security of the new creation rests \u2014 sin will never rise again because every question has been answered.

**The end of evil \u2014 thorough and final.** Ezekiel 28:18-19 and Malachi 4:1 both describe the destruction of the wicked in language of total consumption: ashes, no more forever, neither root nor branch remaining. Adventists understand this as annihilation \u2014 not eternal conscious torment but irreversible cessation of existence. The fire that destroys is real, but it completes its work. God does not perpetuate suffering; He ends it.

**Corrective boundary.** This doctrine guards against two errors: (1) the belief that God's final judgment happens behind closed doors without accountability, and (2) the belief that evil will be eternally preserved in a realm of ongoing torment. Both distort God's character. The millennium reveals a God who is both just enough to end evil and transparent enough to prove it.`,
